---
title: "mikod.com and Code Explorer"
date: "2026-11-11"
summary: "I built a website for source code and a Visual Basic 6 desktop app to go with it, back when Visual SourceSafe was the back end."
topics: ["Development", "Source Control", "Visual Basic"]
---

I started `mikod.com` in what I remember as February 1999. The name was literally “my code dot com.” The idea was to give source code a home on the web. I built a desktop app to go with it called **Code Explorer**, in Visual Basic 6. The first version used Microsoft Visual SourceSafe as its back end.

I still have a copy of Code Explorer from 2001. Alongside the old Windows executables are HTML templates for file information and revision history. Those are the pieces that make me remember what I wanted from the web side. I wanted to be able to look up a file and see how it got to be the way it was.

I used Code Explorer at SunGard in the early 2000s. When I started at LANDesk in 2003, the team used CVS, so I started adapting it. I had the CVS work mostly done when we moved to Subversion. That’s about when I stopped working on the web platform. I kept Code Explorer going for myself for a while, then lost interest in that too.

GitHub [launched in April 2008](https://github.blog/news-insights/we-launched/). Seeing source code and its history become things you could casually browse on the web reminded me how much I’d wanted that in 1999. Of course I’ve wondered what would have happened if I’d kept at it. Could it have become a company the size of GitHub? It’s a fun what-if.

I have a few of those. I used to mine Bitcoin, too, until I got tired of keeping the miners running. I can do the arithmetic on the coins I could have kept. With mikod.com, what I’m still curious about is what I would have built after that CVS port.
